00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:07.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  good morning everyone my name is yudit  and i am somewhat of a youth activist  but really just the true enthusiast of  of peace uh democracy in europe  and uh how my relationship to these  topics changed over time is what i'm  gonna share about here today with you  and i hope many of you can connect with  it and maybe get inspired  for your own journey as well  europe day is a very special day for  many of us  my generation  i was born in 1993  has never or barely known borders on a  large part of the european continent  so we really know  what it feels like to be european and we  have convictions about what europe is  and can be if we are collaborate  some would also say that there are 447  million european citizens  um and i'm sure if you're following this  speech actually most of you are a  european citizen too  but actually that's just a number right  that's the official population of the  european union but there's so much more  uh about european democracy that we need  to uncover  not to mention that actually maybe some  people who should be considered european  citizens are missing from this number  altogether  so  these things really excite me and as a  youth activist and as a professional my  my day-to-day life in essence is  determined by building a peaceful and  democratic europe  i work now as incoming secretary general  of the young european federalists which  is a pan-european youth organization  but  my journey to get here started much  sooner of course  growing up in hungary um i had many  influences around me  i had my local and national realities of  course as a predominant one  but i understood early on that there's  something beyond these realities that is  also impacting my everyday life  for example i also had the presence of  my mom  who was born in at the time called west  germany  because my family is a german speaking  minority in hungary and they fled in  1956  when there was an uprising against the  soviets  and when that ended actually they moved  back to hungary when my mom was 10 years  old  but needless to say i already had a  sense of course of of how there's a  complexity in in our world  um  and a fun fact is that actually my name  was chosen by my mom uh so that it can  be pronounced in multiple languages  maybe this was a predetermination of  what was to come  but  essentially i was growing up in hungary  in an industrial town of 80 000 people  called tatadanya  and no one really taught me anything  about the eu or about europe  no one really taught me how to relate to  this mysterious actor called europe  but then come high school  i was lucky enough to take part in a  series of student exchanges that turned  my life completely upside down  i it opened doors i never imagined  existed  but it also made me reflect on things i  maybe simply did not want to before  but what became unavoidable was put in  contrast with others  therefore my identity brewed in many  different directions during these  teenage years  i went through a period of longing to  live in the netherlands for example  which i had the chance to visit and i  made very deep friendships there but i  also met young people from poland from  germany and also from the united states  and  i had the chance to also host them in my  own home and  because of this actually there was no  turning back for me  in realizing that we are more united  that different  while also celebrating the diversity of  our uniqueness  as  individuals  at the time however i did not think much  about it how we as a community think and  act  i simply left high school i went on to  university and i was committed to stay  part of this international life somehow  whatever that may be  and then another unexpected door opened  i took part in an eight months long  training by my local student union in  community organizing  and i felt that i found my calling  i decided to study educational sciences  because i felt what i enjoyed the most  was helping others to create the best  life for themselves and for others  around them  but little did i know you can also do  this as a volunteer um  and i learned that there and slowly i  started to deliver trainings myself and  supported other young people to to be  well equipped to participate  and on top of that i i did not expect  that actually through supporting other  people  i learned that my own voice matters too  i met wonderful mentors and peers who  cracked me open and allowed me to  express things about myself  that i cared about  and i felt empowered by my communities  to to come forward and and voice  opinions um sometimes these were  these were the ones that we all shared  and sometimes these were only my own  even if it was not the popular opinion  at the time  so from a mix of all of these  experiences a deep belief  in active youth participation emerged  and looking back it is easy to see that  actually i was learning about democracy  and europe  through practice  there i was  reborn in multiculturality and and youth  empowerment  and the natural next step was to combine  these two actually my in my actions  so during and after university i  continued to be a part of  youth and student representation and  multiple international youth  organizations  with whom we took our voices to stand up  for the ideas that we believe in  which is what i am doing to this day  and sometimes you know it happens in the  form of a thunderstorm  you take the streets for social justice  for the planet that we will live on to  be protected or to defend democracy rule  of law or human rights  but sometimes it is more in the form of  how a river is washing the shores and  creating its path in the soil you keep  on engaging with different people in  power positions over and over again  calmly and resiliently  until they take your opinions into  consideration  this is also all part of what youth  participation is  and sometimes you have to really go  again and again about the same problem  addressing the same people and see  barely any change  but sometimes  magic happens  and change does occur  so i say that ejector matters  and young people have been doing all of  it  there's a great amount of us who are  marching who are voting who are running  for elections who are also empowering  other young people to do all of these  same things  active participation in democracy has  many faces  and it can be a roller coaster believe  me  there were times when i was asked to  join certain fights or fulfill certain  positions  and i said no  i sometimes regret these decisions but  then i realized there was a reason at  the time  and i know this because there were also  other times when i took on too much and  i had to prioritize my well-being  because no one can fight for democracy  24 7.  you need to find your balance and your  purpose  at the end of the day in a democracy it  all comes down to what matters to you  and what is the best form for you to  channel it into action  through exchanging with others being  open to hear different perspectives and  respecting each other  we always find a common way forward  and it is becoming increasingly  important to understand that in our  current democracies we all have to keep  the bigger picture  in this case europe and the world  in our minds  otherwise our direct realities get  impacted as well and we have no way to  act around it  globalization and european integration  made all of us more and more dependent  on each other which brought wonderful  opportunities such as the european  solidarity corps program for example or  some other opportunities  but there is still a long way to go  there are many issues that cannot be  faced or solved alone or in fragmented  manners  pandemics do not stop at borders  the climate crisis doesn't happen in  silos  and  we all know that also the impact of war  is always a wider circle than you  anticipated  and needless to say though that human  rights are also universal  and their promotion cannot stop at  borders either  in times where racism xenophobia  homophobia or any other type of  discrimination still persists we still  have a lot to learn from each other  in times where women's rights continue  to have severe restrictions in some  countries  we still have a lot to learn from each  other  and in times where armed conflict  is back in europe  we still have a lot to learn from each  other  but the only way to peacefully do so is  through democracy  is it perfect  no of course not  but it is the best option we have  right now  to provide every european citizen with a  life of dignity  and in this fight  you  me and everyone has a part to play  fundamentally we need strong cooperation  between all actors maybe institutions or  different communities across europe  you know whether you call it federalism  as my organization jeff europe or mario  draghi would do for example  or you call it a geopolitical  eulogy and the president of the european  commission would do matters very little  at the end of the day  they all point to a type of democracy  that fosters peace  diversity and unity  something we need more of at the moment  but on the bright side uh programs like  erasmus bus or the solidarity core that  i mentioned already  are great examples for fostering  democracy in europe and beyond  they realize both the value in investing  in youth  and in learning from each other and  acting in solidarity  this is how we strengthen democracy  this is how we offer a strong way for  europe to go forward  and when this road seems a bit rocky  because some inside hate  and division instead of fostering this  learning we need to step in  and young people are often the first  ones to do so and i'm sure many of you  in this room have done that before  so therefore it's also great to see that  actually 2022 has been recognized as the  european year of youth because i feel  this is a recognition for all the work  that young people have been doing for  the present  and the future  of europe  and  to slowly close i i just hope that each  and all of you feel the strength of your  voice  today and every day  peace and democracy are not a given  they need to be fostered  europe and the world will flourish only  to our actions  and i i did not know this when i was 14.  i had this mysterious sector i didn't  understand who it was right called  europe for the european union  but by being open to new experiences and  seeking to cooperate with others i  learned  that the mysterious europe is what i  make of it  and at the end of the day each one of us  builds europe day by day so if i can do  it  you can do it too  [Applause]

